INSERT INTO page_components (id, page_id, slot_name, position, component_id, content_data, build_status)
SELECT gen_random_uuid(), p.id, 'article-body', 2, cc.id, jsonb_build_object('content', $ARTBODY$<h2>The Question Every Business Is Actually Asking</h2><p>Nobody asks "should we use AI." Most organisations already have. The question that actually gets debated in a boardroom, a hospital IT committee, or a law firm's risk meeting is narrower and harder: should this system see this data. A customer record. A patient chart. A CV. A trading position. The answer depends on what the system is, who built it, what happens to the data afterward, and what happens when it gets something wrong.</p><p>This article, and the three that follow it, try to answer that question honestly — with real numbers from people who asked it at scale, not with a sales pitch in either direction. The evidence does not point one way. Adoption is running well ahead of the governance needed to make it safe, in almost every sector we looked at. It is also true that AI-assisted security teams catch breaches faster and cheaper than teams without it, and that a well-run AI vendor can now document its security posture more thoroughly than most of the businesses trusting it with data ever could on their own. Both things are true at once. This is a map of where the real risk sits, industry by industry, and what actually reduces it — not a verdict.</p><h2>The Trust Paradox, In One Study</h2><p>The most useful single data point on this question comes from the largest study of its kind: a joint effort between the University of Melbourne and KPMG, surveying more than 48,000 people across 47 countries between November 2024 and January 2025.</p><blockquote>"A global study on trust in Artificial Intelligence (AI) released today reveals more than half of people globally are unwilling to trust AI, reflecting an underlying tension between its obvious benefits and perceived risks." — KPMG International / University of Melbourne, <em>Trust, attitudes and use of artificial intelligence: A global study</em>, April 2025</blockquote><p>The numbers behind that sentence are the paradox in full: 66% of people already use AI regularly, and 83% believe it will bring a wide range of benefits — yet only 46% say they are willing to trust it. Two-thirds (66%) rely on AI output without checking whether it is accurate, and 56% say they have made a mistake at work because of it. Among employees, 58% use AI intentionally in their jobs, and 57% admit to presenting AI-generated work as their own without disclosing it. Seventy percent think the technology needs stronger regulation than it currently has.</p><p>That is not a population that has decided AI is untrustworthy. It is a population using it constantly while remaining unconvinced it deserves the confidence it is getting — and, in more than half of cases, quietly hiding that use from the people relying on their work. That gap between use and confidence is the subject of everything that follows.</p><div class="trust-chart" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: percentage who trust AI, by role. General public 46%. Healthcare consumers already using AI for their health 88%, not using it 38%. Hiring managers 70%. Job seekers who call AI hiring fair 8%."><svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto;font-family:'Inter',-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,sans-serif;">
<text x="0" y="20" style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;fill:#1A1A1A;">Who says they trust AI — by who's asking</text>
<text x="0" y="38" style="font-size:11px;fill:#5A5751;">Same technology, very different confidence depending where you sit</text>
<g style="font-size:12px;fill:#1A1A1A;">
<text x="0" y="72">General public, worldwide</text>
<rect x="260" y="58" width="184" height="20" style="fill:#836E32;"/><text x="450" y="73" style="font-weight:700;">46%</text>
<text x="0" y="112">Patients already using AI for health</text>
<rect x="260" y="98" width="352" height="20" style="fill:#836E32;"/><text x="618" y="113" style="font-weight:700;">88%</text>
<text x="0" y="152">Patients not using AI for health</text>
<rect x="260" y="138" width="152" height="20" style="fill:#B9B3A6;"/><text x="418" y="153" style="font-weight:700;">38%</text>
<text x="0" y="192">Hiring managers, in their own tool</text>
<rect x="260" y="178" width="280" height="20" style="fill:#836E32;"/><text x="546" y="193" style="font-weight:700;">70%</text>
<text x="0" y="232">Job seekers, who call it fair</text>
<rect x="260" y="218" width="32" height="20" style="fill:#0D0D0D;"/><text x="298" y="233" style="font-weight:700;">8%</text>
</g>
<line x1="260" y1="50" x2="260" y2="248" style="stroke:#E4DFD5;stroke-width:1;"/>
<text x="0" y="272" style="font-size:10px;fill:#5A5751;">Sources: KPMG/University of Melbourne 2025 (n=48,000+, 47 countries) · Reach3 Insights/Rival Technologies, 2026 Digital Health Trends · Greenhouse, 2025 AI in Hiring study (n=4,136)</text>
</svg></div><h2>Why the Same Technology Reads So Differently Depending Who's Asking</h2><p>Look at the chart above and the pattern is not really about AI at all. It is about who is on the receiving end of the decision. A hiring manager who chose the AI screening tool trusts it at more than eight times the rate of the candidate being screened by it. A patient already using AI for their own health decisions trusts it more than twice as much as one who has never tried it. Trust tracks agency and familiarity — whether you chose the system, and whether you understand what it is doing — far more reliably than it tracks the technology's actual accuracy.</p><p>That single insight explains most of what follows in this series. Every sector's "AI trust problem" turns out to be, on inspection, a question of who is deciding, who is affected, and whether the second group had any say in the first.</p><h2>Healthcare: The Data Is the Most Sensitive, and the Public Is Watching the Wrong Risk</h2><p>Trust in AI use inside healthcare has been falling, not rising, even as adoption climbs. Reach3 Insights and Rival Technologies' 2026 Digital Health Trends report found that only 44% of Americans now trust the use of AI in healthcare, down from 52% two years earlier — with a sharp trust split between people who already use AI for their own health (88% trust it) and people who don't (38%).</p><p>What is notable is what patients are actually worried about. Asked to name their primary concern, 36% cite the accuracy of a diagnosis or treatment recommendation — the risk most coverage of medical AI focuses on. But 30% cite the privacy and security of their health data specifically, and patients surveyed expressed more concern about their health data being commercialised or sold than about algorithmic bias. The public's worry is not only "will it get my diagnosis wrong" — it is "who else gets to see this, and what will they do with it." A 2025 IBM analysis put the average cost of a healthcare-sector security breach at over $7.4 million, the highest of any industry it tracks, and found that 97% of organisations that suffered an AI-related security incident had no proper AI access controls in place at the time.</p><p>The research on what actually builds patient trust is specific and somewhat encouraging: a US survey of 3,000 adults found people were significantly more willing to trust and choose medical AI when it had demonstrably better performance, FDA approval, national or local certification, a clinician present in the loop, and training data that represented people like them. None of those are exotic requirements. They are closer to a checklist than a mystery — which is the subject of our companion piece on healthcare specifically.</p><h2>Financial Services: Adoption Is Real, Governance Is Not Keeping Pace</h2><p>Sixty-three percent of bank employees now use AI weekly, and financial services has the highest AI integration rate of any sector tracked for cybersecurity use (82%, per industry analysis of fraud detection and transaction monitoring deployments). But Deloitte's 2026 banking research found that while 87% of banks say they could significantly improve their AI governance, only 13% consider themselves at a leading maturity stage — and just 31.8% of institutions surveyed have actually deployed AI/ML technology into production, with only 12.2% describing their strategy as "well-defined and resourced."</p><p>The consumer side of this is unforgiving. Salesforce's financial-services customer research (a survey of over 6,000 customers) found 78% would switch providers if they felt their data had been mishandled. That number should recalibrate how any financial firm thinks about the cost of an AI data incident: it is not primarily a regulatory fine, it is the customer relationship itself. Cambridge Judge Business School's 2026 Global AI in Financial Services report notes the industry reported more AI-related incidents in the first half of 2026 than in all of 2025 combined — while data quality and legacy architecture, not caution, remain the leading constraint on scaling AI further, cited by 66% of AI vendors and 46% of regulators as the top barrier.</p><h2>The Regulatory Floor Is Rising Everywhere, Not Just in Finance</h2><p>The EU AI Act's obligations for high-risk AI systems apply in full from 2 August 2026, and they are specific about data: Article 10 requires that training, validation and testing data used in a high-risk system be "relevant, representative, free of errors, and complete to the extent possible," on top of separate requirements for risk management, technical documentation, human oversight and cybersecurity. Non-compliance carries fines of up to €35 million or 7% of global annual turnover, whichever is higher — and the Act applies to any business whose AI affects people inside the EU, regardless of where that business is based. For a UK or US company that has treated AI governance as optional, this is the year it stops being optional for anyone doing business with the EU.</p><h2>Legal and Professional Services: The Danger Is the Informal Use, Not the Sanctioned Tool</h2><p>Thomson Reuters' Future of Professionals research found the share of legal organisations actively integrating generative AI rose from 14% in 2024 to 26% in 2025, with 45% either using it already or planning to make it central to their workflow within a year. The same research found near-unanimous agreement on what a legal AI tool has to do: 96% of professionals say it must safeguard confidential data, 94% say its outputs must be grounded in authoritative content rather than the open web, and 90% say its reasoning must be explainable and defensible if challenged.</p><p>The risk that actually worries people who have looked closely at legal AI is not the sanctioned tool with a signed agreement behind it — it is the unsanctioned one. A prompt typed into a public AI tool can contain personal data, privileged detail, or case strategy; once it leaves the firm's control, an unmanaged data transfer has already happened, agreement or no agreement. That is a training and policy problem as much as a technology one, and it is the same shape of risk found in every sector we looked at: the sanctioned system is usually the well-governed one, and the real exposure lives in the tools nobody signed off on.</p><h2>Hiring: The Widest Trust Gap We Found, By Far</h2><p>Greenhouse's 2025 AI in Hiring study — 4,136 respondents across the US, UK, Ireland and Germany, split roughly evenly between job seekers and hiring professionals — produced the starkest single number in this research:</p><blockquote>"70% of Hiring Managers Trust AI to Make Faster and Better Hiring Decisions, Only 8% of Job Seekers Call it Fair" — Greenhouse, <em>An AI Trust Crisis</em>, November 2025</blockquote><p>Eighty-seven percent of organisations now use AI somewhere in hiring — writing job descriptions, screening resumes, matching candidates, scheduling interviews. Eighty-two percent of hiring managers say they are concerned about candidates using AI in their own job search, an irony that is hard to miss: the same tool is trusted when the employer deploys it and distrusted when the candidate does. Only 35% of companies always disclose their AI use to candidates being evaluated; 20% never disclose it at all. This is our companion piece's subject in full, because it is the case where "whose data, whose decision" is starkest: it is the candidate's data, and overwhelmingly the employer's decision alone.</p><h2>Retail, the Public Sector, and Everyone Else: The Same Trade-Off, Smaller Print</h2><p>Two more findings round out the picture, from sectors that will each get fuller treatment eventually but are worth stating plainly now. In consumer retail, Salesforce's State of the Connected Customer research found only 42% of customers now trust businesses to use AI ethically — down from 58% in 2023 — and only 37% trust brands with their data at all, even as 83% remain willing to share it in exchange for a genuinely better personalised experience. That is not a contradiction; it is a price. Customers are naming what they want in return for the data, and most companies are not delivering it: only 49% of customers who share data believe it is actually being used in ways that benefit them.</p><p>In government, the OECD's 2025-26 Survey on Drivers of Trust in Public Institutions found citizens consistently more confident that AI will make public services faster and cheaper (43% and 42% confidence respectively) than confident it will be used transparently, fairly, or with their privacy protected — and that confidence in those safeguards varies enormously by country, from 38% in Australia to 51% in Switzerland for the same question about protecting personal information. Public trust in AI, in other words, is not one number. It is a specific judgment about a specific government's specific track record, and it moves accordingly.</p><h2>The Case Against: What the Data Actually Says Goes Wrong</h2><p>None of this is abstract risk. IBM's 2025 Cost of a Data Breach Report puts a number on it: global average breach cost fell to $4.44 million in 2025 — the first decline in five years — but US organisations faced a record $10.22 million average. Within that figure, breaches involving "shadow AI" — generative AI tools employees adopted without security sign-off — cost roughly $670,000 more than the average breach, now account for one in five breaches studied, run 6 days longer on average, and are more likely to compromise customer personal data (65% of shadow-AI breaches vs. 53% overall). Eighty-three percent of the organisations studied had no basic controls in place to stop data reaching an AI tool in the first place, and 97% of organisations that suffered an AI-related incident lacked proper access controls specifically. McKinsey's 2026 AI trust research, surveying roughly 500 organisations with direct AI governance responsibility, found only a third meet governance standards adequate for autonomous AI agents — even as those same organisations deploy agentic AI faster than their governance can keep up with, because, as McKinsey puts it, the risk has shifted from a system saying the wrong thing to a system doing the wrong thing: taking an unintended action, misusing a tool, acting beyond its intended scope.</p><div class="trust-chart" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: cost of a data breach in 2025. Global average 4.44 million dollars. US average 10.22 million dollars. Extra cost when shadow AI is involved, 0.67 million dollars on top of the average."><svg viewBox="0 0 720 260" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto;font-family:'Inter',-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,sans-serif;">
<text x="0" y="20" style="font-size:14px;font-weight:700;fill:#1A1A1A;">What a breach costs, 2025</text>
<text x="0" y="38" style="font-size:11px;fill:#5A5751;">Average cost per breach; shadow AI adds to whichever average it touches</text>
<g style="font-size:12px;fill:#1A1A1A;">
<text x="0" y="80">Global average</text>
<rect x="200" y="66" width="196" height="24" style="fill:#836E32;"/><text x="404" y="83" style="font-weight:700;">$4.44M</text>
<text x="0" y="128">US average</text>
<rect x="200" y="114" width="450" height="24" style="fill:#0D0D0D;"/><text x="658" y="131" style="font-weight:700;">$10.22M</text>
<text x="0" y="176">Extra, when shadow AI is involved</text>
<rect x="200" y="162" width="30" height="24" style="fill:#B9B3A6;"/><text x="238" y="179" style="font-weight:700;">+$0.67M</text>
</g>
<line x1="200" y1="58" x2="200" y2="196" style="stroke:#E4DFD5;stroke-width:1;"/>
<text x="0" y="220" style="font-size:10px;fill:#5A5751;">Source: IBM, Cost of a Data Breach Report 2025</text>
</svg></div><h2>The Case For: What the Data Says Actually Works</h2><p>The same report that documents that damage also documents the counter-case for AI, measured rather than assumed: organisations that used AI and security automation extensively cut their breach discovery time by 80 days and reduced their average breach cost by $1.9 million compared to organisations that did not. Deployed AI fraud-detection systems now process transaction volumes — one documented system handles 12 million transactions a day — while cutting false positives by 73% and catching 91% of genuinely fraudulent activity, a level of throughput no manual review process could match. Cisco's 2025 Data Privacy Benchmark Study, surveying 2,600 privacy and security professionals across 12 countries, found 96% now say their organisation's privacy investment returns exceed its cost, and 86% actively support privacy legislation rather than merely tolerating it — evidence that treating data governance as a cost centre is increasingly a minority view among the people who actually run it.</p><p>The productivity case is real but narrower than the marketing suggests. PwC's 2026 AI Performance Study found that in industries most exposed to AI, productivity growth nearly quadrupled — from 7% (2018–2022) to 27% (2018–2024) — but that three-quarters of that economic value is currently captured by just one-fifth of companies, the ones treating AI as a reason to rebuild how they work rather than a way to trim the workforce doing the old work faster. Fifty-six percent of CEOs surveyed reported neither increased revenue nor reduced costs from AI in the past year. The gap between those two groups is not the technology. It is whether data governance, human oversight and a defined scope were built in from the start, or bolted on after something went wrong — which is precisely the KPMG finding this article opened with, restated as a business result instead of a survey response.</p><h2>What "Trustworthy" Actually Looks Like, Concretely</h2><p>Enough of this is now measurable that "do you trust this AI vendor with your data" has a checklist behind it, not just a feeling. Anthropic's own published trust documentation is a reasonable model of what that checklist contains in practice: SOC 2 Type I and Type II attestations, ISO 27001:2022 certification for information security management, ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — a certification specifically for the management of AI systems, distinct from general information security — and a HIPAA-ready configuration with a Business Associate Agreement available for healthcare customers. On the data-handling side: as of its policy update in October 2025, customer conversations are not used to train models by default, with an explicit opt-in required to change that; and for regulated workloads, customers can request Zero Data Retention, under which prompts and outputs are not stored beyond what is needed to serve the request at all.</p><p>None of that guarantees good outcomes on its own — certifications describe a process, not a promise, and the KPMG study found 66% of people still don't check AI output for accuracy regardless of what sits behind it. But it is a genuinely different category of evidence than a vendor's word, and it is now realistic to ask any AI system touching your data for the equivalent list: what's certified, what's retained, what's used for training, and what happens if something goes wrong. A vendor that cannot answer those four questions specifically is telling you something, whatever else it says.</p><h2>Where This Series Goes Next</h2><p>The pattern across every sector in this piece is the same one visible in the opening chart: trust follows agency, familiarity, and disclosure — not the sophistication of the model. The three articles that follow take that pattern into the sectors where the stakes, and the evidence, are sharpest: <a href="/blog/ai-data-trust-in-healthcare.html">how healthcare is handling the most sensitive data category there is</a>, <a href="/blog/ai-data-trust-in-financial-services.html">why financial services is racing to scale AI faster than it can govern it</a>, and <a href="/blog/ai-data-trust-in-hiring-and-hr.html">why hiring produced the widest trust gap in all the research we reviewed</a>.</p><h3>Sources cited in this article</h3><ul><li>KPMG International &amp; University of Melbourne, <em>Trust, attitudes and use of artificial intelligence: A global study</em>, April 2025 (n=48,000+, 47 countries)</li><li>Pew Research Center, <em>Americans and AI 2026: Chatbots, Smart Devices and Views on Impact</em>, survey Feb 17–23, 2026</li><li>Edelman, <em>2026 Edelman Trust Barometer</em>, Global Report, January 2026</li><li>Cisco, <em>2025 Data Privacy Benchmark Study</em> (n=2,600, 12 countries)</li><li>McKinsey, <em>State of AI Trust in 2026: Shifting to the Agentic Era</em> (n≈500 organisations)</li><li>IBM, <em>Cost of a Data Breach Report 2025</em></li><li>Reach3 Insights &amp; Rival Technologies, <em>2026 Digital Health Trends</em></li><li>Deloitte, <em>Banking on trust: AI governance for growth, resilience and scale</em>, 2026</li><li>Cambridge Judge Business School, <em>2026 Global AI in Financial Services Report</em></li><li>Salesforce, financial-services customer research (n=6,058) and <em>State of the Connected Customer</em> research</li><li>Thomson Reuters, <em>Future of Professionals</em> report, 2026</li><li>Greenhouse, <em>An AI Trust Crisis: AI in Hiring</em>, November 2025 (n=4,136)</li><li>OECD, <em>Survey on Drivers of Trust in Public Institutions</em>, 2025–26 results</li><li>PwC, <em>2026 AI Performance Study</em> / Global AI Jobs Barometer</li><li>European Union, <em>AI Act</em>, high-risk system obligations effective 2 August 2026</li><li>Anthropic, Trust Center (trust.anthropic.com) and "What certifications has Anthropic obtained?", Claude Support</li></ul>
$ARTBODY$), 'deployed'
FROM pages p, content_components cc
WHERE p.site_id='4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732' AND p.name='can-you-trust-ai-with-your-data' AND cc.name='article-body';

INSERT INTO page_components (id, page_id, slot_name, position, component_id, content_data, build_status)
SELECT gen_random_uuid(), p.id, 'call-to-action', 3, cc.id, $CTAJSON${"headline": "Building something that has to handle real data responsibly?", "subheadline": "We design agent systems with the audit trail, human-approval points, and data-handling discipline this article describes, not bolted on afterward. Contact us at leopardess@contactforsales.com or call +44 (0) 7934 524 911.", "primary_cta": "Start with an architecture conversation", "secondary_cta": "See how the platform runs in production"}$CTAJSON$::jsonb, 'deployed'
FROM pages p, content_components cc
WHERE p.site_id='4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732' AND p.name='can-you-trust-ai-with-your-data' AND cc.name='call-to-action';

SELECT p.name, p.id AS page_id, count(pc.id) AS n_components
FROM pages p JOIN page_components pc ON pc.page_id=p.id
WHERE p.site_id='4851f6fc-71cf-4160-a270-e03d6d3e0732' AND p.name='can-you-trust-ai-with-your-data'
GROUP BY p.name, p.id;

COMMIT;
